## Recommended: Use `System.IO.Abstractions`

The best approach for `File.*` and `Directory.*` is the established **System.IO.Abstractions** NuGet package — no need to write custom wrappers.

### 1. Install packages

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class ConfigManager(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool OutputExists(string dir)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(dir);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});
mockFs.AddDirectory("/output");

var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", manager.Load("/data/config.json"));
Assert.True(manager.OutputExists("/output"));

manager.Save("/data/new.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/new.txt"));
```

### Why this over a custom wrapper?

| Custom wrapper | System.IO.Abstractions |
|---|---|
| You maintain it | Community-maintained, battle-tested |
| Covers only what you write | Covers the full `System.IO` surface |
| Must build your own mock | Ships `MockFileSystem` with test helpers |

This gives you full testability with minimal effort. Once injected, your classes have zero direct static dependencies on the file system.
